An object is handed out holding what its memory held before the pool took it,
since pool_alloc no longer zeroes it.
Boot zeroes the boot pool, so the root task starts clean,
but a pool made later lies on RAM that holds a pattern on the host,
as it holds anything at power-up:
a table's slots, a thread's links and a process's regions start as garbage,
so the first table the replay's prologue allocates holds capabilities
that nothing its caller held covers.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: a call made a capability no capability of its caller covers

diff --git a/kernel/boot.c b/kernel/boot.c
--- a/kernel/boot.c
+++ b/kernel/boot.c
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@ struct granted_range boot_granted[GRANTED_RANGES];
 struct thread *boot_create_root(void)
 {
     /* Its own node is a root until the Untyped it lies below is in the table. */
+    memset(p2v(BOOT_POOL_BASE), 0, BOOT_POOL_SIZE);
     struct pool *pool = pool_create(BOOT_POOL_BASE, BOOT_POOL_SIZE, NULL);
 
     struct captable *table = pool_alloc(
diff --git a/kernel/pool.c b/kernel/pool.c
--- a/kernel/pool.c
+++ b/kernel/pool.c
@@ -152,8 +152,6 @@ void *pool_alloc(struct pool *pool, uint8_t type, size_t size)
     uint32_t aligned = align_up((uint32_t)size, OBJ_ALIGN);
     pool->used += aligned;
     /* The memory holds what it held before the pool took it; the padding is zeroed with the rest. */
-    CALL_BOUND(OBJ_MAX_SIZE + OBJ_ALIGN);
-    memset(obj, 0, aligned);
     obj->type = type;
     obj->pool = pool_base(pool);
     obj->back = (uint16_t)((v2p(obj) - pool->last) / OBJ_ALIGN);
